 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The traversal is hierarchical DDA with pixel-footprint LOD [S1, unofficial]. The complexity depends on the depth of the octree and the number of visible voxels. The use of a job queue allows for parallel processing on the GPU [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for inner nodes, leaf nodes, animation matrices, and the BVH [S1, S2, unofficial]. The BVH is rebuilt per frame, which adds overhead but allows for efficient culling of animated parts [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The traversal follows a hierarchical DDA with pixel-footprint LOD [S1, unofficial]. The traversal complexity depends on the depth of the octree and the number of visible voxels [S2, unofficial]. The use of a job queue allows for parallel processing on the GPU [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for inner nodes, leaf nodes, animation matrices, and the per-frame BVH [S1, S2, unofficial]. The per-frame BVH allows for efficient culling of non-visible animated parts [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Performance:** The implementation includes unit tests for traversal and BVH against brute force, and a `--compare` mode that checks GPU output against the CPU renderer, ensuring correctness [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficiently handles animated voxel models using skinning and hierarchical culling. The per-frame BVH allows for efficient culling of non-visible animated parts.
+*   **Strengths:** Efficiently handles animated voxel models using skinning and hierarchical culling [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Limitations:** The per-frame BVH construction adds overhead. The method is specific to OpenGL 4.5 compute shaders in the provided implementation [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-ASVO was introduced as part of a thesis project on animated sparse voxel octrees [S2, unofficial]. The original implementation was in CUDA/C++ with an XNA/C# prototype for design [S2, unofficial]. A later rebuild by Andre Rogers added an OpenGL 4.5 compute shader implementation with X-Toon shading, fixing several shader bugs from the original 2016 version [S1, unofficial]. The method is demonstrated with scenes such as a crowd of walkers sharing limb octrees and a bonsai tree [S1, unofficial].
+ASVO originated as a thesis project on animated sparse voxel octrees [S2, unofficial]. The initial high-performance implementation used CUDA/C++ with an XNA/C# prototype for data structure and algorithm design [S2, unofficial]. A later rebuild by Andre Rogers introduced an OpenGL 4.5 compute shader implementation with X-Toon shading, fixing several shader bugs from the original 2016 version [S1, unofficial]. The method is demonstrated with scenes such as a crowd of walkers sharing limb octrees and a bonsai tree [S1, unofficial].
+
+## Subtopics
+
+### Voxel Animation Playback
+Animates imported voxel scenes using keyed transforms (nTRN) and frame-by-frame model switching (nSHP) [S3, unofficial]. Tracks nTRN frame keys for transform animation (rotation and translation) and nSHP model keys for switching [S3, unofficial]. Driven by a root VoxelAnimationPlayback3DComponent clock [S3, unofficial]. Parameter: `autoplay` boolean in Model3DSource controls automatic playback start [S3, unofficial].
 
